-- 319(3), (3.1), (3.2): defences. Made out: JUST TRUE; the charge is the named offence and the defence is not made out: JUST FALSE, "this section gives no defence on these facts"
-- (the ruling of 2026-11-05, (b)); another charge: NOTHING. The repealed paragraphs (b) are carried as strings.
-- src:6049 | Defences
-- src:6050 | (3) No person shall be convicted of an offence under subsection (2)
-- src:6051 | (a) if he establishes that the statements communicated were true;
-- src:6052 | (b) [Repealed, 2026, c. 15, s. 4]
-- src:6053 | (c) if the statements were relevant to any subject of public interest, the discussion of which was for the public benefit, and if on reasonable grounds he believed them to be true; or
-- src:6054 | (d) if, in good faith, he intended to point out, for the purpose of removal, matters producing or tending to produce feelings of hatred toward an identifiable group in Canada.
@ref s 319(3) (src:6049-6054)
GIVEN `the charge is an offence under subsection 319(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person establishes that the statements communicated were true` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the statements were relevant to any subject of public interest, the discussion of which was for the public benefit` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person on reasonable grounds believed the statements to be true` IS A BOOLEAN
      `in good faith, the person intended to point out, for the purpose of removal, matters producing or tending to produce feelings of hatred toward an identifiable group in Canada` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 319(3) the person is not to be convicted of an offence under subsection (2)` MEANS
    IF NOT `the charge is an offence under subsection 319(2)`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "No person shall be convicted of an offence under subsection (2)"
    ELSE IF `the person establishes that the statements communicated were true` OR (`the statements were relevant to any subject of public interest, the discussion of which was for the public benefit` AND `the person on reasonable grounds believed the statements to be true`) OR `in good faith, the person intended to point out, for the purpose of removal, matters producing or tending to produce feelings of hatred toward an identifiable group in Canada`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if he establishes that the statements communicated were true", "(c) if the statements were relevant to any subject of public interest ... and if on reasonable grounds he believed them to be true", "(d) if, in good faith, he intended to point out" (none holds: this section gives no defence on these facts)

